From ffa4685ca6c83b4473dc50623df05f02f96daeb8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hangdong Zhao <67406267+hdz284@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 23:24:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] perf(runtime): fold batch min and max in a hash table

Min and max ignore repeated rows, so batch mode already skips their
dedup (parent commit). Their arrangement still sorted every row only to
keep one value per group. Fold them in a hash table on the worker that
owns each group instead:

- `Semiring::IDEMPOTENT` marks min and max. Present aggregates with an
  idempotent semiring, in loops and in the final recursive fold, go to
  `hash_fold`, which emits each group's answer at each completed time,
  in key order, as the threshold did.
- A zero-sized key (a global aggregate) is folded per batch before the
  exchange, so its single owner receives one weight per batch.
- The lift consumes one input batch at a time, instead of holding all
  of a timestamp's batches beside their lifted copy.
- Count, sum, and average keep dedup plus arrangement: after the dedup,
  a hash table measured no faster and used up to 1.3x the memory.
- The profiler's plan counts the hash fold's operators.

Measured at 32 workers against main (medians of interleaved runs):

  harness, min and max, 6 shapes   2.8-5x faster, 66-96% memory
  harness, count and sum           unchanged; global up to 1.08x faster
  cc, livejournal/orkut/arabic     2.0-2.5x faster, 39-74% memory
  LDBC IC13, SF3                   1.7x faster, 69% memory
  sssp, livejournal                1.16x faster, same memory

Against the parent commit, min and max ran 1.5-2.8x faster in 87-101%
of its memory. Outputs of cc, sssp, and IC13 are identical to main.

